        "title": "Third culture kids",
        "text": "In a world where international careers are becoming commonplace, the number of third culture kids (TCKs), children who spend a significant part of their developmental years in a culture outside their parents' passport culture(s), is increasing exponentially. And it is not only their number that is growing: so are the cultural complexity and relevance of their experience, and of the adult TCKs (ATCKs) they become.\n\nWhen the sociologist Ruth Hill Useem first coined the term in the 1950s, she spent a year researching expatriates in India. She discovered that people who came from their home (or first) culture and moved to a host (or second) culture had in fact formed a culture, or lifestyle, that was different from both the first and the second cultures. She called this the third culture, and the children who grew up in this lifestyle \"third culture kids\". At that time, most expatriate families had parents from the same culture, and they often stayed in one host culture while they were overseas.\n\nThis is no longer the case. Take, for example, Brice Royer, the founder of TCKid.com. His father is a half-French, half-Vietnamese UN peacekeeper, and his mother is Ethiopian. Before he was eighteen, Brice had lived in seven countries, including France, Mayotte, La Réunion, Ethiopia, Egypt, Canada and England. He writes: \"When people ask me 'Where are you from?', I just joke around and say, 'My mom says I'm from heaven.'\" What other answer could he give?\n\nThe father of ATCK Elizabeth Dunbar, Roy, moved from Jamaica to Britain as a young boy. Her mother, Hortense, was born in Britain, the child of Jamaican immigrants who always planned to go back home \"one day\". Although Elizabeth began life in Britain, her father's international career took the family to the United States, then to Venezuela, and then back to three different cities in the US. She soon realised that while racial diversity may be recognised, the hidden cultural diversity of her life remained invisible.\n\nDespite such complexities, however, most ATCKs say that growing up among different cultural worlds has given them many priceless gifts. They have seen the world and have often learnt several languages. More importantly, through friendships that cross the usual racial, national or social barriers, they have also learned the very different ways people see life. This gives them a great opportunity to become social and cultural bridges between worlds that traditionally would never connect. ATCK Mikel Jentzsch, author of Bloodbrothers – Our Friendship in Liberia, a best-selling book in Germany, has a German passport but grew up in Niger and then Liberia. Before the Liberian civil war forced his family to leave, Mikel played every day with children who were later forced to become soldiers in that war. Through his eyes, the stories of people we would otherwise overlook come to life for the rest of us.\n\nUnderstanding the TCK experience matters for other reasons too. Many ATCKs now hold positions of influence and power. Their ability to think \"outside the box\" can offer new and creative thinking for doing business and living in our globalising world. But the same thinking can create fear among those who see the world from a more traditional point of view. Neither the non-ATCKs nor the ATCKs may realise that there may be a cultural clash going on, because by traditional measures of diversity, such as race or gender, they are alike.\n\nIn addition, many people hear the benefits and challenges of the TCK profile described and wonder why they identify with it, when they never lived overseas because of a parent's career. Usually, however, they have grown up cross-culturally in some other way, perhaps as children of immigrants or refugees, children of bi-racial or bi-cultural unions, international adoptees, or even children of minorities. If we see the TCK experience as a kind of Petri dish, a place where the effects of growing up among many cultural worlds together with a high degree of mobility have been studied, we can then look for lessons that may also help us understand issues that other cross-cultural kids (CCKs) may face. We may discover that we need to rethink our traditional ways of defining diversity and identity. For some people, as for TCKs, \"culture\" may be defined by shared experience rather than by shared nationality or ethnicity. By telling their stories and developing new models for our changing world, many will be able to recognise and make good use of the great gifts of a cross-cultural childhood, and deal successfully with its challenges, for their personal, communal and corporate good."
